WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.060
This is a story of success.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.930
Amongst the hundreds of thousands of Central
Europeans who left Europe to go to America

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:10.030
in search of a better life, many are those
who found out that America’s promises were

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:16.039
vacuous, many managed to establish a decent
living and some achieved spectacular successes,

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:20.390
demonstrating that this was the right country
to welcome a young immigrant with the right

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.619
work ethic, unique talents and a sharp business
acumen.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:28.390
This is the story of the success of a Hungarian
boy who went to America and became one of

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.609
the most successful entertainers of all times,
known as the Master of the Escape.

00:00:33.609 --> 00:00:39.059
He was, of course, Harry Houdini – acrobat,
contortionist, magician, escape artist, entrepreneur,

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:43.469
film tycoon, debunker of occultists, maybe
even a secret agent …???

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.920
Above all, Harry Houdini was a master marketer
and manager of his own public image, using

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:55.329
a variety of media to promote his shows and
his famous stunts that would defy Death and

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.469
leave it empty handed every time.

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:05.840
Please welcome Harry Houdini, the closest
America ever got to having a real life superhero.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:17.609
Erik
Our hero was born on the 24th of March 1874

00:01:17.609 --> 00:01:22.109
in Budapest, Austro-Hungarian Empire, with
the name of Erik Weisz.

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:24.890
[Pronounced as ‘Vice’]
He was one of seven children born to Samuel

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.509
and Cecilia, who had married in 1863.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:32.200
Samuel [TA1] had started in life as a soapmaker
and he certainly had intellectual ambitions:

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.159
he graduated as a Doctor in Law and had also
studied to become a Rabbi and Bible scholar,

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:40.729
eventually becoming a civil servant in Budapest.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:44.750
His career must have hit a hard stop, though,
as the Weisz family was forced to emigrate

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.479
to the USA in 1887, in search of fortune and
a better life.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:53.770
This ‘hard stop’ according to some sources,
was an event worthy of a novel by Alexandre

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.130
Dumas, father of the Three Musketeers.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.899
It seems that Samuel had challenged – and
killed – in a duel an Austro-Hungarian nobleman,

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:03.729
known only as ‘Erich’.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.109
To prevent prosecution, he had to organise
an escape to America [TA2] with the help of

00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:11.190
a good friend called Carl Herman, whom you
may find amusing to know, was at that time

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.080
one of the most famous stage magicians in
Europe.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.110
It is foreshadowing, to say the least, that
the early life of he who would become Harry

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:23.200
Houdini were to be influenced by an escape
aided by a stage magician.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.200
This version of the events may be corroborated
by the fact that according to other sources,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.030
Samuel first left alone for the States[TA3]
, followed by the rest of the family at a

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.070
later stage.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.570
Whatever the reasons for this escape, or emigration,
the Weisz had their surname changed to ‘Weiss’

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:38.730
in Ellis Island.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.760
The ever-creative immigration officers also
re-christened young Erik as ‘Ehrich’.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.110
Ehrich[TA4]
Young Ehrich and his family initially lived

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.580
between Appleton and Milwaukee, in Wisconsin.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.880
Ehrich demonstrated from a very early age
a talent for athleticism, becoming very early

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.770
a proficient runner, swimmer and amateur boxer.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:04.260
A History Channel mini-series, aired in 2014
portrayed Ehrich’s relationship with his

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.570
father as fractured and dysfunctional.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.450
In reality, Ehrich was very closed to Samuel,
who encouraged Ehrich’s early interest in

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.890
acrobatics and stage magic by taking him to
Circus and Carnival shows.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.230
In 1883 in fact Ehrich started his career
as a performer, becoming a contortionist.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.440
He was able to perform feats such as bending
over backwards in a ‘bridge’ position

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.530
to pick up a pin from the floor with his teeth.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.890
He also learned very early how to dislocate
shoulders, or other joints, from their sockets

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.970
in order to increase his flexibility.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:37.950
These early displays of stagecraft were absolutely
not opposed by Samuel and Cecilia.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.850
The Weisses found it difficult to make a living:
Samuel’s career as a Rabbi had not fully

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.950
picked up since the move to America, and any
job that Ehrich and his siblings could pick

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.120
up were very welcome to the family finances.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.310
This was demonstrated by a sad event.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.150
On Christmas Eve 1885, the oldest of the Weiss
siblings, Herman, died of tuberculosis at

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.150
the age of 22.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.980
And it was Ehrich who paid for the funeral
expenses, thanks to his earnings as a performer.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.370
When Ehrich turned 13 he and Samuel moved
to New York.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:09.780
It was here that the pre-teen added acrobatics
to his talents, performing as a trapeze artist

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.730
with stage names such as ‘Prince Erik’
or ‘The Prince of the Air’

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.840
[TA5] During the same period young Ehrich
would pick up another talent that would become

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.190
key to his later career.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:23.190
He became the apprentice of a locksmith, a
line of work which made him become fascinated

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.180
with locks of any kind – and also how to
pick them open.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.750
It is reported that he developed such skills
that he stunned his employer in at least two

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:33.380
occasions: like that time when he managed
to open a pair of handcuffs using only a piece

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.960
of piano wire; or when he unlocked all the
doors to all the shops in his street … just

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:38.970
for the hell of it!

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:43.430
With the combination of these talents - acrobatics,
contortionism, lock-picking - Ehrich could

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.030
have easily gone into becoming a burglar.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.820
And who knows, I may have been here anyways
talking about how he had pulled the most successful

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.160
heist in History.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:56.220
But luckily, he soon found another career
to channel his talents, surely more honest

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:02.810
but by any means no less risky.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.690
Harry
At the age of seventeen Ehrich and his brother

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:11.470
Theo worked on developing a magical act[TA6]
, which they would perform at so called ‘Penny

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:16.710
Circuses’, music halls, sideshows and at
New York’s Coney Island amusement park.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:21.100
As they grew in popularity Ehrich thought
it wise they chose a stage name.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.139
Ehrich and Theo used to call each other ‘Harry’
and ‘Dash’.

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:26.040
So, first names sorted.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.440
As per the last name, Harry went for ‘Houdini’,
after one of his idols, French illusionist

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.440
Robert Houdin.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.710
[Who-dan]
The Houdini Brothers would perform up to 20

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:34.970
shows every single day.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.880
Their partnership continued until in 1894
they met another entertainer, teenage singer

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.770
and dancer Wilhelmina Beatrice Rahner, better
known as Bess.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.639
At that time, Bess was part of an act called
“The Floral Sisters” and had initially

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:52.420
started dating ‘Dash’, but after she and
Harry met they were smitten with each other.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:53.750
It was a real coup de foudre.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.040
Harry and Bess courted, got engaged and married
in the span of one week!

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.710
In addition to be life partners they also
became partners on stage, as Bess replaced

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:02.940
Dash as Harry’s sidekick.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:08.740
So, at this point of the story we should really
feel sorry for Dash Houdini aka Theo Weiss.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:13.260
The poor guy has been basically pushed to
the side twice by his brother, romantically

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.500
and professionally.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.139
No wonder then, that the two would develop
a bitter rivalry, right?

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:18.139
Well, wrong again.

00:06:18.139 --> 00:06:23.090
But I am not going to leap ahead like a trapeze
artist now, so I am going to stick to Bess

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:27.820
and Harry’s love story.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:33.220
Bess
Bess and Harry remained deeply in love for

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.640
the rest of their lives – and maybe even
after.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.512
Harry gave her credit for many of his achievements
and developed the habit of writing her a love

00:06:39.512 --> 00:06:40.890
letter every single day.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.590
Sometimes, when they were on tour, Harry would
write her a letter even when sitting in the

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:45.930
same hotel room.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.810
It is widely reported that the couple was
largely faithful to each other, despite the

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:54.280
temptations that a famous magician may incur
when meeting his fans.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.960
Well actually Harry did not stick 100% to
his marital vows: he had at least one confirmed

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:05.290
and documented affair with a lady called Charmian
London, wife of famous novelist Jack London.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.540
Bess, on the other hand, was much more serious.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:12.280
Only on one occasion, to make Harry jealous,
she sat on a lap of a young Colonel at a party.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.840
The ruse worked: Houdini, the man who would
defy death thousands of times on and off stage,

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.360
was terrified at the sight of his wife sitting
on some other guy’s lap.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.570
For months afterwards, the mere mention of
the word ‘Colonel’ would get him into

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:24.570
a funk.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:28.170
In 1895, the Houdinis joined the Welsh Brothers
Circus for six months.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.900
Harry performed his magic tricks while Bess
sang and danced.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.790
Together they devised an illusion called "Metamorphosis",
in which they switched places in a locked

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.490
trunk, as fast as the bat of an eyelid.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.840
Over the same period Harry and Bess wowed
audiences by performing as spiritualists on

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:46.880
stage – in other words acting as mediums
to summon the spirits of the dead.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:51.000
It was all an act, of course, but performed
very convincingly: Harry apparently got Bess

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.020
into a trance and then the spirits would use
her as a mouthpiece to communicate with the

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.020
living.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.530
Harry’s favourite approach was to ask the
spirits about recent unsolved murders in the

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:00.540
town they were performing.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.260
It is said that on one occasion one of such
spirits revealed the identity of a murderer.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.360
The man, who was sitting among the audience,
jumped from his seat and fled the scene[TA7]

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:09.990
.
Was the story true, or just one of the many

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.790
publicity stunts that Harry would pull over
the years?

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:18.889
PR became one of the many skills Harry mastered,
alongside his most visible and memorable endeavour:

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:20.630
the art of escape.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:29.610
The King of Handcuffs
When arriving in a new town, Houdini issued

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.539
a challenge to the local police department:
he would be able to escape from any handcuffs

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:34.899
provided by the local officers.

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:37.680
If he failed, he would offer a reward of $ 100.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.740
As you may have guessed, he never had to pay.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.160
This type of stunts would bring him increasing
attention from the press and free promotion

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:49.350
for his shows, to the point that he became
a headliner on the vaudeville circuit.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.760
In 1899 Harry got his first big break.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:57.339
A vaudeville impresario called Martin Beck
encouraged Houdini to focus his shows on escapes

00:08:57.339 --> 00:09:01.480
– any type of escape from handcuffs, chains,
locks, trunks and shackles.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.750
Beck also booked him on a tour of Europe,
which would be a turning point in the magician’s

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.750
career.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:10.670
The following year Mr and Mrs Houdini sailed
to England for their first engagement in a

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:11.730
London theatre.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:17.110
As ever, he insured his success with a clever
PR stunt: Houdini wowed audiences and ‘bobbies’

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:22.119
alike when he successfully broke free from
handcuffs placed on him by arguably the best

00:09:22.119 --> 00:09:25.160
police department in the world, Scotland Yard.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:29.569
According to authors William Kalush and Larry
Sloman, this stunt impressed the head of Scotland

00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:35.079
Yard, William Melville who initiated Houdini
to his parallel, secret career: that of a

00:09:35.079 --> 00:09:38.119
spy in His Majesty’s Service.

00:09:38.119 --> 00:09:40.140
Was Houdini a spy?

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.769
The idea is so enticing that I want a mini-series,
right here, right now, about Houdini spying

00:09:43.769 --> 00:09:47.249
on the French, the Russians, the Germans in
the years leading to WWI.

00:09:47.249 --> 00:09:51.850
The claim is, after all, credible: as a famous
performer Harry would have been able to tour

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.160
all of Europe undisturbed and have access
to high ranking officials.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.680
And if he ever got caught … good luck in
restraining him!

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.670
No, he wasn’t
However, the theory has been debunked by several

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:04.670
other authors.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:08.629
First of all, the British Intelligence Service
was not established until 1909.

00:10:08.629 --> 00:10:13.290
Then, by 1900 Houdini was not yet famous enough
to presume that he would be able to, say,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:16.019
perform for the Kaiser or anything similar.

00:10:16.019 --> 00:10:21.329
But the best counter argument comes from fellow
magician and professional debunker, the Great

00:10:21.329 --> 00:10:22.329
James Randi.

00:10:22.329 --> 00:10:25.879
“If Houdini had been a spy, that would have
gotten out.

00:10:25.879 --> 00:10:29.821
He never would have been able to sit on it!”[TA8]
Let’s go on with the tour[TA9]

00:10:29.821 --> 00:10:33.430
In November of 1900 Harry invited his brother
Dash [TA10] to join him in the rest of his

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:34.730
European tour.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.730
Was this a reconciliation for the two brothers?

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:41.060
Actually, there was never a rift between them
and the two had always been and always remained

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:42.350
close friends and collaborators.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:47.139
Harry had a plan: he had helped Dash in launching
his career as a magician and escape artist

00:10:47.139 --> 00:10:51.220
with the stage name of Hardeen, which sounded
close enough to Houdini.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:55.519
As Harry and Bess toured the European theatres,
Hardeen would follow suit and book performances

00:10:55.519 --> 00:10:57.759
in venues that Harry didn’t play.

00:10:57.759 --> 00:11:02.160
This was a scheme to shut out of the market
the many cheap imitators of Harry’s escape

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:05.949
art, at the same time raking in additional
revenue that would stay in the family.

00:11:05.949 --> 00:11:10.869
The supposed rivalry was also part of the
ruse: the two brothers simply fed this fake

00:11:10.869 --> 00:11:14.910
piece of news to the press, knowing very well
that this created even more free advertising

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:15.910
for their shows.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:20.160
In at least one occasion Dash, Theo, Hardeen,
whatever you like to call him, even pioneered

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.230
an act that would become one of Harry’s
best-known routines: the strait jacket escape.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:29.459
Meanwhile, the publicity from the Scotland
Yard escape had caused Houdini's fame to spread

00:11:29.459 --> 00:11:30.529
across the Channel.

00:11:30.529 --> 00:11:34.529
Sold-out engagements followed in Germany and
then across other European countries.

00:11:34.529 --> 00:11:38.829
In addition to leaving police forces baffled,
Harry came up with another way to attract

00:11:38.829 --> 00:11:43.230
attention: jumping into rivers, fully chained
and handcuffed.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:48.100
With sadistic glee, Houdini would remain underwater
long after having removed his shackles, allowing

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:50.300
the suspense to build among the audience.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:55.319
With his last remaining breath, he would then
emerge from under water waving triumphantly.

00:11:55.319 --> 00:12:01.800
This was performance art, guerrilla marketing
and viral promotion all rolled into one.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:09.529
Death defying acts[TA11]
In 1905 Harry and Bess returned to the United

00:12:09.529 --> 00:12:10.529
States.

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:13.639
He had become an international celebrity by
now and the public were expecting increasingly

00:12:13.639 --> 00:12:15.600
daring exploits from him.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:17.360
And he satisfied them.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:19.410
I already mentioned the strait-jacket act.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:23.990
Harry was able to set himself free from such
a contraption thanks to his ability to self-dislocate

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:28.329
his shoulders from their sockets, in pure
Martin Riggs style

00:12:28.329 --> 00:12:29.329
[Subtitle: If you got that reference, you
are too old for this sh*t]

00:12:29.329 --> 00:12:33.990
But that was too easy, so Houdini would perform
the act while hanging upside down from a skyscraper

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.269
(!) in the cities he was touring in.

00:12:36.269 --> 00:12:37.510
And not just any skyscraper.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:42.239
As we have already established that the man
was a marketing and PR pioneer: so, he picked

00:12:42.239 --> 00:12:44.550
buildings that housed major newspapers.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:48.490
This ensured that photographers got the best
shots of him and The Great Houdini would make

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.749
the headlines.

00:12:49.749 --> 00:12:51.950
Unsurprisingly, his shows were sold out.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:56.290
His triumphant return to America was the setting
for the creation of increasingly outrageous

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:57.589
death-defying acts.

00:12:57.589 --> 00:13:01.769
You have probably heard about, or seen images,
of the most famous ones.

00:13:01.769 --> 00:13:03.720
For example, the milk can escape.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.959
Houdini was shackled and lowered into an industrial
size milk can filled with water, and then

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:10.489
hidden behind a curtain.

00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:14.360
Though he was usually able to escape in three
minutes, Harry frequently stayed behind the

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:18.290
curtain for up to a half hour, making his
re-appearance even more dramatic.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:22.480
The milk can was the setting for one of his
rare failures: in one performance the can

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.639
was filled with beer, rather than water.

00:13:24.639 --> 00:13:28.309
That sounds like great fun to you and me,
but Houdini was a tee-totaller and the effects

00:13:28.309 --> 00:13:29.850
of alcohol knocked him out.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:33.250
For once, he had to be pulled to safety by
his stage hands.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.050
Another famous example was the act he called
‘Upside Down’, but which the press rebranded

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.000
as ‘Chinese Water Torture Chamber’.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.720
Houdini would have his feet shackled, his
hands handcuffed and then would be placed

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.819
upside down inside a transparent tank filled
with water.

00:13:45.819 --> 00:13:50.009
To increase the suspense, he sometimes invited
the audience to hold their breath for as long

00:13:50.009 --> 00:13:52.209
as it took for him to emerge from the Chamber.

00:13:52.209 --> 00:13:56.410
One less known, but much more bizarre stunt
was a one-off event that took place in September

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:58.059
1911 in Boston.

00:13:58.059 --> 00:14:03.619
Some businessmen challenged Houdini to attempt
an escape from inside the carcass of a 1,500-pound

00:14:03.619 --> 00:14:06.540
“sea monster” that had washed up in Boston’s
harbour.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:10.259
It is not clear what the monster was, maybe
a whale or a giant leatherback turtle.

00:14:10.259 --> 00:14:12.300
In any case, Houdini accepted the challenge.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:17.110
In front of thousands of spectators Harry
was handcuffed, shackled and wedged inside

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:18.329
the stinking carcass.

00:14:18.329 --> 00:14:22.540
Houdini emerged after 15 minutes – normally
he would have done with the irons much more

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:26.949
quickly, but he was nearly poisoned by the
chemicals used to embalm the monster.

00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:33.160
He was by now one of the top celebrities in
America and for sure the most famous stage

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:34.670
magician slash master lock picker.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:35.670
But there is more to life than just
stage magic

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:36.670
and picking
locks[TA12]

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:39.019
But there is more to life than just stage
magic and picking locks and Harry’s vivid

00:14:39.019 --> 00:14:43.209
curiosity did not allow him to stagnate on
just one talent.

00:14:43.209 --> 00:14:48.019
In the 1910s and early 1920s he sought to
diversify his activities, driven by patriotism,

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:53.629
love of adventure, love of justice or simply
to make a few extra bucks – which are all

00:14:53.629 --> 00:14:54.629
perfectly fine motivations.

00:14:54.629 --> 00:14:59.619
During a 2nd tour of Europe in 1909 Harry
had developed a love for aviation and had

00:14:59.619 --> 00:15:03.560
bought a Voisin biplane, Made in France.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.850
Interesting to note – he was one of the
first private pilots.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:10.499
In March 1910 Houdini was touring Australia
and took three short flights with his Voisin,

00:15:10.499 --> 00:15:11.499
near Melbourne.

00:15:11.499 --> 00:15:14.819
This was certified at the time as the first
powered flight to take place Down Under, making

00:15:14.819 --> 00:15:20.069
Harry Houdini a pioneer of aviation – plus
I am sure it got him some extra headlines

00:15:20.069 --> 00:15:21.240
and ticket sales along the way.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:22.240
[Subtitle: it was later discovered that he
had been beaten by a couple of months by a

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.529
British aviator, Colin Defries]
A few years later and back in the States,

00:15:23.529 --> 00:15:26.429
Harry volunteered to serve the Government
in a time of need.

00:15:26.429 --> 00:15:31.569
In April 1917 the US entered the War against
the Central Empires and although Houdini was

00:15:31.569 --> 00:15:36.470
theoretically Austro-Hungarian, he was staunchly
loyal to his country of adoption.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:41.619
In 1917 and 1918 he cancelled his tours to
entertain soldiers instead, and to raise money

00:15:41.619 --> 00:15:42.619
for the war effort.

00:15:42.619 --> 00:15:46.350
He even organised some lessons for the troops,
where he taught them how to free themselves

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.319
if they were made captive by the Germans.

00:15:48.319 --> 00:15:51.249
Not all his extra-magician activities were
successful, though.

00:15:51.249 --> 00:15:55.069
Between 1919 and 1923 Houdini tried his hand
at movie stardom.

00:15:55.069 --> 00:15:59.079
He was the lead in “The Master Mystery”,
before deciding to create his own production

00:15:59.079 --> 00:16:00.779
company with the help of Dash.

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:04.889
The Houdini Picture Corporation released “The
Man from Beyond” and “Haldane of the Secret

00:16:04.889 --> 00:16:05.889
Service”.

00:16:05.889 --> 00:16:07.490
The latter two films tanked at the box office.

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:11.079
It seems like Harry, after all, could fail,
too.

00:16:11.079 --> 00:16:15.859
Anyways, by the beginning of the 1920s Houdini
had other preoccupations on his mind.

00:16:15.859 --> 00:16:19.879
This is when he started a parallel career
as debunker [TA13] of occultist, spiritualists

00:16:19.879 --> 00:16:20.929
and mediums.

00:16:20.929 --> 00:16:24.869
In general, he would expose as fakes anybody
who claimed to communicate with the dead – which

00:16:24.869 --> 00:16:27.389
is ironic given his early performances.

00:16:27.389 --> 00:16:32.910
What Houdini could not tolerate was the exploitation
of well-meaning individuals, willing to communicate

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:34.519
with their deceased loved ones.

00:16:34.519 --> 00:16:40.100
He campaigned with fervour, often visiting
séances in disguise to expose their ringleader

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.100
as frauds.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.660
When he could not go in person, he sent one
of his team of detectives who would go to

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:49.850
seances on his behalf and bring back information
… information which allowed him to set up

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:54.100
shows entirely dedicated to exposing the tricks
used by the fake mediums.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:58.879
Houdini offered a $10,000 reward to any psychic
who could present “physical phenomena”

00:16:58.879 --> 00:17:01.470
that could not be explained rationally.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:06.130
The psychics were to be investigated by a
committee from the Scientific American publication.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:10.870
One of the mediums under the lens was well-known
spiritualist Mina Crandon, known as ‘Margery’.

00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:14.870
Margery attempted to seduce several members
of the Committee to secure the prize, and

00:17:14.870 --> 00:17:16.620
she even made a pass at Harry.

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:19.130
Houdini was not easily charmed, as he said
of her

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:24.450
‘Her applesauce means nothing to me’
Houdini’s crusade culminated in 1926, when

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:29.600
he testified before the US Congress in support
of a bill to outlaw the practice of “pretending

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:32.140
to tell fortunes for reward or compensation”.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:35.620
The bill was made into law, albeit only in
the District of Columbia.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:40.230
I should clarify now that Houdini’s debunking
efforts were not entirely based on pure scepticism.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:45.600
In fact, Harry did believe in a spiritual
world, he simply did not trust charlatans

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.310
who could claim access to it.

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:50.870
His belief in a Thereafter was put to the
test by an experience which involved one of

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:53.809
his dearest friends, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:17:53.809 --> 00:17:58.010
Sir Arthur’s main creation Sherlock Holmes
was a paladin for rational thinking, and yet

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.340
the author firmly believed that the dead could
talk to the living.

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:05.850
Lady Doyle shared this belief and had a reputation
for being an accomplished medium.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:11.340
In 1922 Sir Arthur and Lady Conan Doyle organised
for Houdini an automatic writing seance that

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:16.240
supposedly contacted his beloved mother Cecilia,
who had died nine years earlier.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:20.029
Cecilia’s spirit convincingly took control
of Lady Doyle’s body – but Houdini was

00:18:20.029 --> 00:18:21.250
not easily fooled.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:24.280
Why did the wife of a Rabbi do the sign of
the cross?

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:29.389
Why a Hungarian immigrant who never learned
English, suddenly was writing in that language?

00:18:29.389 --> 00:18:32.810
This seance was the beginning of the end of
Houdini's friendship with Sir Arthur Conan

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:38.190
Doyle – and may have fuelled his animosity
towards mediums.

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:44.809
Halloween, 1926[TA14]
While touring in Montreal, Canada, Houdini

00:18:44.809 --> 00:18:48.169
was visited by two students of McGill University
in his dressing room.

00:18:48.169 --> 00:18:51.270
It was the 22nd of October 1926.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:55.130
One of them, J. Gordon Whitehead, asked Harry
if he could really absorb any punch to the

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:56.130
stomach.

00:18:56.130 --> 00:19:00.390
Harry replied that yes, he could, and the
student suddenly punched him four times, probably

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:03.440
having misunderstood the reply as an invitation
to do so.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:07.600
Houdini had terrible stomach pain for two
days, before finally seeing a doctor.

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:10.100
Harry was found to have a high fever and acute
appendicitis.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:14.820
He was advised to go to the hospital for surgery,
but he preferred to go on stage and complete

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:15.820
his show.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:19.330
But by the middle of the third act, Houdini
lowered the curtain and collapsed.

00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:22.559
Bess and Dash had him taken to the hospital
where his appendix was removed.

00:19:22.559 --> 00:19:25.400
But it was too late: it had already ruptured.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:31.789
On October 31 – Halloween – 1926, Harry
Houdini died, Bess and Dash at his bedside.

00:19:31.789 --> 00:19:36.809
The official cause of death: acute peritonitis,
caused by the appendix being ruptured by an

00:19:36.809 --> 00:19:37.970
ill-timed punch.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:41.409
You may have heard the theory that Houdini
was actually the victim of a spiritualist

00:19:41.409 --> 00:19:45.039
conspiracy, but of course there is no evidence
of that.

00:19:45.039 --> 00:19:49.960
Houdini's funeral was held on November 4,
1926 in New York with more than 2,000 mourners

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:50.960
in attendance.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:54.940
In the years that followed Bess held annual
séances every Halloween to contact Harry.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:58.940
The last séance took place exactly 10 years
after Harry’s death, and it was broadcast

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:00.080
over the radio.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:04.650
After over an hour of silence Mrs. Houdini
decided to end the experiment, as Harry’s

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.760
spirit would just remain silent.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.710
As the séance came to an end, a violent thunderstorm
broke out.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:14.090
The participants, sitting on a hotel roof
top terrace were drenched by the violent rainfall,

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.230
while lighting set the sky ablaze.

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:20.149
They would later learn that the storm did
not occur anywhere else in the area - only

00:20:20.149 --> 00:20:21.559
above the séance location.

00:20:21.559 --> 00:20:25.960
I would not be surprised to learn if this
was Harry’s final stunt to wow yet another

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:40.470
audience and say goodbye - with a bang! – to
his beloved Bess.

